Contrast this with `threads-and-the-shared-memory-model`'s racy counter — same logical operation, structurally impossible to race:

```scala
class CounterActor extends Actor {
  private var count = 0   // private — nothing outside this actor can touch it

  def receive: Receive = {
    case Increment     => count += 1
    case GetCount(ref) => ref ! count
  }
}
```

No lock, no `synchronized`, no `AtomicInteger` — and no race is possible, because messages are processed one at a time by definition. A thousand `Increment` messages sent concurrently from a thousand different senders still get handled sequentially by this single actor; the mailbox does the serialization the earlier topic needed a primitive for. This is the actor model's core selling point made concrete: the race condition from two topics ago isn't fixed here, it's **inexpressible**.
